IDiscussionBoardConfiguration.description:
System-wide configuration setting for the discussion board platform. This schema represents a single configuration key-value pair that controls platform behavior, moderation policies, user experience parameters, and operational constraints. Configuration values are stored in the discussion_board_configurations database table and can be modified dynamically without code deployments.

The configuration system enables administrators to adjust platform parameters through administrative interfaces, affecting functionality across the entire platform. Each configuration record is uniquely identified by its key field and contains a value that may be a string, number, boolean, or JSON-encoded configuration object depending on the setting.

This schema is used across multiple API operations: as the response type for GET /configurations/{configKey} to retrieve single settings, as the response type for GET /configurations to list multiple settings, as the request body for POST /configurations to create new settings, and as the request body for PUT /configurations/{configKey} to update existing settings.

Important: All configuration values are stored as strings in the database and may be parsed to their appropriate types by consumers. Sensitive settings related to moderation thresholds, authentication limits, and internal system parameters are restricted to administrator access only.

Changes to configuration values are direct and immediate updates to the database record. The current value is always the latest value stored in the database with no soft delete behavior or historical versioning.

Usage Example:
- {"key": "max_comments_per_post", "value": "25", "description": "Maximum number of comments allowed per post"}
- {"key": "enable_anonymous_posts", "value": "true", "description": "Whether anonymous posting is allowed on the discussion board"}
- {"key": "content_moderation_level", "value": "{\"severity\": \"high\", \"auto_flag_threshold\": 5}", "description": "Configuration for automated content moderation settings"}

context.description:
Optional structured data that provides context for the notification trigger. This object contains references to related entities (like article ID, comment ID, or user ID) that the notification needs to reference. The structure of this object varies depending on the triggerType.

For example:
- For "POST_PUBLISHED": { "article_id": "art-123" }
- For "COMMENT_REPLY": { "comment_id": "com-456", "parent_article_id": "art-123" }
- For "REPORT_SUBMITTED": { "report_id": "rep-789", "target_content_id": "art-123" }

This allows the system to customize notification messages using the referenced entity data and ensures proper linkage with related content. This field is critical for the notification system to determine recipients, personalize message content, and maintain audit trails of content relationships. Even though this field is technically optional in the object structure, it is required in the schema because all notification triggers must provide context for compliance, tracking, and system functionality.

